# SwimAI — Workflow Rules (Claude.ai + Cursor)

## Prompt discipline — max 3 tasks per prompt
- NEVER send a Cursor prompt with more than 3 distinct file changes
- If a feature needs more changes, split into sequential prompts
- Each prompt must be independently buildable (pnpm build passes after it)
- Rationale: long prompts cause agent timeouts, partial execution, and hard-to-debug failures

## Audit → Plan → Confirm → Build (mandatory sequence)
1. AUDIT ONLY prompt first — never modify files in an audit prompt
2. Claude presents a plan based on audit findings
3. Fred confirms the plan before any implementation prompt is sent
4. Implementation prompt references the confirmed plan
- NEVER skip the plan confirmation step for features touching 3+ files
- Exception: single-file bug fixes can go straight to implementation

## Report handling
- Fred saves Cursor's response as a .txt or .md file and uploads to Claude.ai
- Never paste Cursor reports directly — they consistently render as empty documents
- If a report uploads empty, Claude reads it directly using the view tool

## Browser verification (mandatory before commit)
- Every implementation prompt ends with explicit browser verification steps
- Fred verifies in real browser before telling Claude to commit
- Claude never writes the git commit command until Fred confirms browser verification
- No exceptions — "build passes" is not a substitute for browser verification

## End-of-session ritual (last 5 minutes)
1. git add -A && git commit && git push
2. Claude updates swimai-build-retrospective.md
3. Fred re-uploads .cursorrules to Claude Project if it was modified
4. Fred re-uploads retrospective to Claude Project

# SwimAI — Project Rules

## Stack
- Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, shadcn/ui
- Package manager: pnpm only. Never suggest npm/yarn commands or generate
 package-lock.json/yarn.lock.
- Icons: lucide-react only.
- CSS: Tailwind v4 with `@import 'tailwindcss'` + `@tailwindcss/postcss`
 PostCSS plugin. No tailwind.config.ts — design tokens live in `app/globals.css`.

## Content & i18n
- All user-visible copy lives in `messages/fr.json` (primary) and
 `messages/en.json` (secondary), accessed via next-intl's `useTranslations()`
 hook. Never hardcode user-visible strings directly in JSX.
- `lib/content.ts` is deprecated/unused — do not add new copy there, and flag
 it for removal if you encounter it.
- French is the primary/default locale. Every new string added to `fr.json`
 must have a matching key added to `en.json` in the same change.
- Never invent translations silently — if unsure of an English phrasing, flag
 it rather than guessing.

## Components
- One component = one file, PascalCase filename matching the default export.
- Functional components only, typed props via an explicit `interface` (not inline
 object types), e.g. `interface CoachCardProps { coach: Coach }`.
- No inline `style={{...}}` objects except for things Tailwind genuinely cannot
 express (e.g. dynamic background-image URLs, gradient stops with alpha values).
 Everything else must use Tailwind utility classes.
- No raw hex/oklch colors in component files. All colors must reference the
 design tokens defined in `app/globals.css` (e.g. `bg-teal-accent`,
 `text-text-secondary`, `fill-star-gold`).
 - This rule applies to `.tsx` component files and any other non-CSS files.
 - `app/globals.css` is the token source of truth and is expected to contain
 raw hex/oklch values in its `:root` and `.dark` variable blocks —
 that is correct, not a violation.
- Shared types (`Coach`, `NavLink`, etc.) live in `/types`, never
 re-declared inline in multiple components.

### Content-card design system (canonical glass-card recipe)
- ONE canonical surface treatment for content cards app-wide:

```
rounded-3xl border-blue-300/20 bg-blue-400/[8%] shadow-xl
shadow-black/20 backdrop-blur-md
```

- Applied to: coach cards, pricing cards, for-coaches benefit cards, footer
 strip, testimonial card, dashboard stat/booking/message/schedule cards,
 dashboard sidebar + content frame, onboarding shell containers, login/signup
 form containers, coach profile page panel, coaches-listing filter sidebar.
- Documented exceptions — do NOT apply this recipe to these, they are
 intentionally distinct:
  - Status-coded cards (`components/dashboard/coach-pending-requests.tsx`)
    — teal/amber/faded-white color-coding is semantic (accepted/pending/
    declined). Converting these would erase meaningful state signaling.
  - Dashed-border "add new item" affordances (e.g. coach schedule's
    "Add slot" button) — dashed border + transparent bg is a deliberate
    "click here to add" UX convention, distinct from content cards.
  - Signup `RoleCard` components (`app/[locale]/signup/page.tsx`) — hover
    state intentionally shifts teal to match the client/coach role icons
    inside; do not normalize to a single hover color.
  - Featured/headline callouts that need to stand out from sibling cards
    (the for-coaches "Inscription et 3 premières réservations" callout,
    How It Works' Step 3 card) — these keep a distinct teal-gradient
    identity (`border-teal-accent/40 bg-gradient-to-r from-teal-accent/15
    via-teal-accent/5 to-transparent`) precisely BECAUSE they need to
    visually outrank their neighbors. Don't "fix" this inconsistency —
    it's load-bearing hierarchy.
  - Floating popovers, the shared Dialog, and filter/selection pills —
    see their own existing sections below; these were never part of the
    content-card system to begin with.
  - Fixed sidebar on the coaches listing page (`components/coaches/coaches-listing-client.tsx`)
    — `backdrop-blur-md` intentionally OMITTED. The sidebar uses `position: fixed` which
    forces a separate GPU compositor layer. Combined with React repaints on filter state
    changes, `backdrop-blur-md` on a fixed element causes a visible horizontal flash line
    across all blurred elements on the page. Fix: remove blur, compensate with
    `bg-blue-400/[15%]` (higher opacity). All other glass cards remain unaffected.
    This is a browser compositing limitation, not a design decision.
- When adding a NEW content card anywhere in the app, use this recipe by
 default. Only deviate if the card needs one of the above documented
 exception reasons — and if so, document the new exception here too.

## Routing
- Every `<Link>` or button that implies navigation must point to a real route —
 never `href="#"` or an empty `onClick`. If the destination page doesn't exist
 yet, create a minimal stub page rather than leaving a dead link.
- Routes follow the locale-prefixed structure: `/[locale]/page-name`.
- **Documented exception:** `href="#"` on "Subscribe" (S'abonner) is accepted
 pending real payment infrastructure. "Become a coach" (Devenir coach) was
 removed from this exception list — `/signup/coach` and the full coach
 onboarding chain (`/onboarding/coach` → profile → availability → done) exist
 and work end-to-end; both "Devenir coach" CTAs now link there directly.
 Any OTHER new `href="#"` must be explicitly flagged in a comment.
- `proxy.ts` (the Next.js 16 middleware file) MUST import and use
 `NextRequest` from `next/server` — never the generic Web API `Request`
 type. Using `Request` (even with `as any`) causes next-intl's middleware to
 fail silently, returning 404 on all locale-prefixed routes (`/fr`, `/en`,
 etc.) with a ~1300 ms hang before the 404. Correct signature:
 ```ts
 import type { NextRequest } from "next/server"
 export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
   return handleI18nRouting(request)
 }
 ```
- Before adding a `href="#"` placeholder with a "pending infrastructure"
comment, verify the claim is still true — check whether the actual
destination (signup page, onboarding flow, etc.) already exists and
works. Placeholder comments go stale the moment the real
infrastructure ships; an unreviewed `href="#"` can sit for many
checkpoints after its blocking reason has already been resolved.
- Hash-only anchors (`href="#search"`, `href="#pricing"`, etc.) must
NEVER be routed through next-intl's `<Link>` — next-intl's `Link`
calls `getPathname()` internally, which has no defined behavior for
a bare hash string (not a registered route). The shared `Button`
component (`components/ui/button.tsx`) has a guard clause that
detects `href` values starting with `#` and renders a native `<a>`
instead, bypassing next-intl's router entirely — any new component
that renders its own hash-anchor links (not through `Button`) must
replicate this same native-`<a>` bypass, or the anchor will silently
fail to scroll.

### Buttons (canonical CTA system)
- ONE Button component: `components/ui/button.tsx`
- Three variants, all `rounded-xl` (NEVER `rounded-full` pills, NEVER ad-hoc
 inline button styling):
 - `variant="primary"` (teal) — "perform the action": search, book, confirm,
 save, submit, continue
 Text color: `text-navy-deep` (#0a1f3d) — dark navy, NOT white. The teal
 background is bright enough that white text feels harsh; navy-deep provides
 strong contrast while feeling intentional against the mint surface.
 `navy-deep` token is defined in `app/globals.css`.
 - `variant="entry"` (blue) — "enter / start the journey": Commencer, Dashboard,
 Become a coach, Subscribe
 Text color: `text-white` — blue-accent is dark enough for white text.
 - `variant="ghost"` (transparent) — tertiary actions
 Text color: `text-white`.
- The shape (`rounded-xl`) is shared across ALL variants. Color signals role,
 shape signals "this is a button." Never use `rounded-full` or pill-shaped
 buttons.
- Supports optional `icon` (left) and `iconRight` (right) props (lucide-react
 components). Supports `href` prop for polymorphic rendering via Next.js Link.
- Language switcher is a segmented control, NOT a Button — it's a setting
indicator, not an action.
- A pill/navbar container that needs to be WIDER than its content
(not width-hugging) must NOT combine `justify-between` with a
width-hugging class (`w-fit`) — `justify-between` requires excess
width to distribute; `w-fit` provides none, causing all children to
collapse together with no visible gap. Two real incidents hit this
exact mistake on the same navbar component.
- To genuinely center ONE item among several while the other items
hug the container's edges (e.g. nav links centered, logo left,
CTA+switcher right, all within ONE wider pill), `justify-between`
+ `gap-*` is insufficient — it centers content WITHIN remaining
space after edge items claim theirs, not within the full container.
Use a CSS grid instead: `grid-cols-[auto_1fr_auto]` with the center
item's column using `justify-self-center` — this guarantees true
mathematical centering regardless of how wide the edge items are.
- CAUTION: a grid's middle column can behave unexpectedly when its
only content is conditionally hidden (`hidden lg:flex` etc.) at
certain breakpoints — if edge-alignment breaks specifically BELOW a
breakpoint where the center content disappears, consider a hybrid:
plain `flex` + `ml-auto` (which reliably pushes trailing content to
the true edge regardless of whether a middle item exists) BELOW the
breakpoint, switching to the `grid` centering technique only AT/ABOVE
the breakpoint where the center content is actually visible (e.g.
`flex ... lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[auto_1fr_auto]`).
- A pill/header that needs to never touch the true viewport edges at
narrow widths (while still capping at a maximum width on wide
screens) should use `max-w-[min(Xrem,calc(100vw-Yrem))]` rather than
a plain `max-w-[Xrem]` — this guarantees a minimum edge margin of
`Y/2`rem on each side at any viewport width, shrinking gracefully
below the cap instead of ever reaching the screen edge.

## Images
- All raster images go through `next/image` (`<Image />`) with explicit width,
 height, and alt text — never a bare `<img>` tag.
- Decorative/background images that must be CSS `background-image` (not `<Image />`)
 should be marked `aria-hidden="true"` and use the optimized WebP version of the
 asset (not PNG/JPG) — see `public/underwater-hero.webp` as the existing pattern.
- New image assets must be compressed/converted to WebP before being added to
 `/public`. Flag any asset over ~200 KB for review before committing.

## Accessibility
- Semantic HTML required: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<footer>`
 — not generic `<div>` soup for structural elements.
- All interactive elements must be keyboard-reachable and have visible focus
 states.
- Decorative-only elements (background images, icon-only flourishes) get
 `aria-hidden="true"`.

### Cursor and focus-visible states (systemic, not per-element)
- EVERY clickable element needs `cursor-pointer` explicitly — contrary to
 common assumption, native `<button>` elements do NOT get a pointer cursor
 by default (the CSS UA default is `cursor: default`). This must be set
 explicitly on every button, and on any non-button element with an `onClick`
 handler (e.g. a `motion.div` used as a dismiss backdrop).
- The canonical focus-visible treatment for native buttons (NOT the shared
 `Button`/`Pill`/`SelectTrigger` components, which have their own established
 rings):

 ```
 focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2
 focus-visible:ring-teal-accent/60
 ```
- Disabled buttons intentionally KEEP their focus-visible ring — do not add a
 `disabled:focus-visible:ring-0` override. A focusable-but-disabled element
 should remain keyboard-discoverable (W3C guidance); only
 `pointer-events-none`/`opacity-40` communicate the disabled state, the
 focus ring stays.
- When adding any new native `<button>` or `motion.button`, include both
 `cursor-pointer` and the focus-visible ring from day one — don't rely on a
 later audit to catch it.

## Established Patterns

### Animations
- Framer Motion is used for the dashboard sidebar (motion.div width
transitions, AnimatePresence for label fade/slide). Follow this existing
pattern for any new animated UI — do not introduce a different animation
approach (e.g. CSS keyframe animations for sidebar collapse/expand).
- When combining Base UI's `Dialog` (or any Base UI primitive with
its own open/close state) with Framer Motion's `AnimatePresence`
for enter/exit animations, the `Dialog.Portal` MUST have the
`keepMounted` prop. Without it, Base UI removes the Portal's DOM
subtree immediately when `open` becomes `false` — before
`AnimatePresence` gets the chance to play the exit animation. The
symptom is: enter animates smoothly, but exit/close snaps shut
instantly with no transition at all. With `keepMounted`, Base UI
keeps the subtree in the DOM regardless of `open` state, and the
outer `{condition && (...)}` React conditional (which
`AnimatePresence` is watching) becomes the sole mount/unmount
authority — Base UI's own `data-closed`/`data-ending-style`
attributes are exposed as CSS hooks but impose no default hidden
styling, so they don't fight the motion component's own opacity/
transform animation.
- Framer Motion's `exit` prop can carry its OWN `transition` nested
inside the exit value object (e.g. `exit={{ x: "100%", transition:
{ duration: 0.35, ease: "easeIn" } }}`) — this is the correct way
to give the exit animation different timing than the enter
animation (which uses the separate top-level `transition` prop),
rather than trying to detect direction and conditionally swap a
single transition object.

### Dialogs / Modals
- Use `components/ui/dialog.tsx`, which wraps Base UI's
 `Dialog.Root / Portal / Backdrop / Popup`. This is NOT Radix.
 Do not use a "Positioner" API — it does not exist in this setup.

### Auth
- `useAuth()` from `lib/auth/auth-context.tsx` is the single auth source.
Any component that needs the logged-in user's data MUST be a `'use client'`
component that reads `useAuth()` directly. Never hardcode a placeholder
name or email "for now."
- Any auth guard that redirects a logged-out user to `/login` MUST
use the actual current path (`usePathname()` from `@/i18n/navigation`)
as the `redirect` query value — NEVER a hardcoded destination like
`/dashboard`. A hardcoded redirect loses the user's original deep-
linked destination (e.g. `/dashboard/coach/schedule` → bounced to
generic `/dashboard` after login instead of back to `/schedule`).
- Dev-only quick-login shortcuts (e.g. `components/dev/fake-auth-debug.tsx`)
do NOT automatically inherit the real login form's `?redirect=`
handling just because they call the same `login()` function —
`login()` is a pure state mutation with no navigation side effect.
Any component offering a login shortcut must explicitly read
`searchParams.get("redirect")` and `router.push()` itself, mirroring
the production `LoginForm.handleSubmit` pattern, or it will silently
do nothing when reached via a redirect bounce.

### Drawer closing animation — drawerClosing pattern
When combining Base UI Dialog Portal with Framer Motion AnimatePresence
for exit animations, use the following pattern to ensure the exit animation
plays fully before the Portal unmounts:

1. Declare a `drawerClosing` state alongside `drawerOpen`:
   `const [drawerClosing, setDrawerClosing] = useState(false)`

2. Keep the Portal mounted during animation:
   `<DialogPrimitive.Root open={drawerOpen || drawerClosing}>`

3. Watch BOTH states in AnimatePresence:
   `{(drawerOpen || drawerClosing) && (...)}`

4. On close — flip `drawerOpen` false IMMEDIATELY (AnimatePresence detects
   exit and starts animation), then flip `drawerClosing` false after the
   animation duration:
   `setDrawerClosing(true)`
   `setDrawerOpen(false)`
   `setTimeout(() => setDrawerClosing(false), animDuration + 50)`

5. Apply `drawerClosing` state to trigger button className so it stays
   visually active during the closing animation:
   `className={cn(...) && drawerClosing ? "teal" : "default"}`

This pattern is used in: `components/header.tsx` (nav drawer) and
`components/coaches/coaches-listing-client.tsx` (mobile filter drawer).

### next-intl API (important gotchas)
- `i18n/navigation.ts` exports `{ Link, redirect, useRouter, usePathname }`
 as named exports only — no default export. Always use `import { Link } from ...`
 (never `import Link from ...`).
- `useTranslations()` does not expose a `.locale` property. To get the current
 locale in a client component, call `useLocale()` from `next-intl` separately.
- In server components, use `getTranslations` from `next-intl/server` (async).
 In client components, use `useTranslations` from `next-intl` (sync).
- next-intl's router (from `i18n/navigation`) auto-prefixes the current
 locale on `push()` / `Link href`. ALWAYS pass UNPREFIXED paths
 (e.g. `/coaches/sophie-chen`, not `/fr/coaches/sophie-chen`). Mixing a
 prefixed path with next-intl's router produces double-locale URLs like
 `/fr/fr/coaches/sophie-chen`. If you need to navigate without locale
 handling, use `next/navigation`'s router instead — but that's almost
 always wrong in this app since pages are locale-prefixed.
- next-intl supports localized URL path segments via the `pathnames` config
 in `i18n/routing.ts` (e.g. `/coaches` → `/recherche` in FR, `/search` in EN).
 Once `pathnames` is configured, EVERY route not explicitly listed in the
 `pathnames` map fails strict type-checking on `Link`/`router.push`. If you
 add a new top-level route, add it to the `pathnames` map (even as
 `'/new-route': '/new-route'` for an unlocalized route) or every existing
 `Link`/`router` call elsewhere may start failing to type-check.
- Dynamic segments (e.g. `/coaches/[id]`) require the OBJECT form when
 navigating: `{ pathname: "/coaches/[id]", params: { id } }` — a plain string
 path is rejected once `pathnames` is configured.
- For locale-switching ON a dynamic route, pass BOTH `pathname` (from
 `usePathname()`) AND `params` (from `useParams()`) to `router.replace()` —
 omitting `params` loses the dynamic segment when switching locale.

### useSearchParams() — Suspense boundary required
- ANY component that calls `useSearchParams()` (from `next/navigation`) MUST
  be wrapped in a `<Suspense fallback={null}>` boundary in its parent page.
  Without it, Next.js 16 App Router throws a 500 on that route.
- The canonical pattern: extract the component body into an inner function
  (e.g. `function LoginPageInner()`), then make the default export a thin
  shell that wraps it:
  ```tsx
  export default function LoginPage() {
    return (
      <Suspense fallback={null}>
        <LoginPageInner />
      </Suspense>
    )
  }
  ```
- This applies to every new page added to the app. Before shipping any new
 page, check whether it (or any component it renders) calls
 useSearchParams() — if yes, the Suspense wrapper is mandatory.
- Pages already fixed: login, signup, signup/client, signup/coach,
 onboarding/client/done, onboarding/client/location, onboarding/coach/done,
 coaches (listing page).

### shadcn defaults in dark mode
- shadcn/ui components ship with `dark:` variant classes that have
 HIGHER specificity than plain Tailwind utility classes (e.g.
 `dark:bg-input/30` on `SelectTrigger`, dark-mode default backgrounds on
 popover surfaces, etc.).
- When overriding shadcn styling in dark-themed UI, plain `bg-transparent`
 will NOT override `dark:bg-input/30` — Tailwind's `dark:` variant
 always wins.
- Always add explicit `dark:` prefixed overrides:
 `dark:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent
 dark:data-[state=open]:bg-transparent`.
- If the library uses non-Tailwind CSS (e.g. react-day-picker's `rdp-*`
 classes) with their own dark mode rules, use `dark:!utility` (!important)
 to win over them.
- Base UI's `Select` and `Popover` share the same `modal` prop but have
 OPPOSITE defaults: `Select.Root`'s `modal` defaults to `true` (locks page
 scroll while open), `Popover.Root`'s defaults to `false` (page stays
 scrollable). If a `Select` dropdown unexpectedly locks page scroll while a
 `Popover` next to it doesn't, this is why — set `modal={false}` explicitly
 on the shared `components/ui/select.tsx` wrapper (already done) rather than
 passing it per call site.

### Floating popover design system
- The app has ONE canonical visual treatment for floating popover
 surfaces (dropdowns, calendar pickers, future tooltips, etc.):

 ```
 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[8%]
 shadow-xl shadow-black/20 backdrop-blur-md text-white
 ```
- Hover/focus state for items inside these popovers:
 `focus:bg-white/10 focus:text-white`.
- Source of truth: `components/date-picker-field.tsx` popover,
 `components/search-bar.tsx` `SelectContent`.
- When adding new floating UI (any new popover, dropdown, or tooltip),
 match this exact treatment. Do NOT introduce a new visual style for
 floating surfaces — consistency across these elements is the design
 system contract.
- When a Base UI `Popover.Root` needs to be closed programmatically
 (e.g. after the user selects a value inside it), make it controlled:
 add `const [open, setOpen] = useState(false)` and pass
 `open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}` to `<Popover.Root>`. Then call
 `setOpen(false)` (or `setTimeout(() => setOpen(false), 150)` for a
 brief visible confirmation before closing) inside the selection handler.
 An uncontrolled `Popover.Root` has no API to close it from inside its
 own content.

### Customizing third-party components
- When a third-party component (react-day-picker, shadcn primitives,
 Base UI) doesn't behave as needed, prefer REPLACING the offending part
 with custom JSX over fighting the library's internal CSS.
- Example: react-day-picker v10's Nav was structurally hard to reposition
 via CSS. Fix: pass `hideNavigation={true}` and render a custom header
 with our own buttons. Took one round; CSS-only patches took six rounds
 and never fully worked.
- Sign you should switch from "patch the library" to "replace the part":
 if you've made 2+ CSS attempts and each produces a different failure
 mode, the architecture is the problem, not the styling.
- When a Base UI component renders its value/label display separately from
 its option list (e.g. `Select.Value` vs. `Select.Item`), do NOT assume the
 value display auto-resolves a label from the selected option. Base UI's
 `<Select.Value>` renders the RAW stored value by default; it only shows a
 translated/formatted label if you pass `children` as a function:
 `<Select.Value>{(value) => translateLabel(value)}</Select.Value>`. Passing
 `undefined` as children does NOT trigger a placeholder fallback once a real
 value is set — Base UI considers any non-null value "selected" and skips
 the placeholder branch entirely.
- react-day-picker outside days (days from the previous/next month shown
 in the current month grid):

 To show them as visible but unselectable, combine TWO things:

 - `showOutsideDays` prop on `<DayPicker>` (makes the numbers render)
 - A `disabled` prop that blocks selection:
   ```tsx
   disabled={(day) =>
     day.getMonth() !== month.getMonth() ||
     day.getFullYear() !== month.getFullYear()
   }
   ```

 Style them via the `outside` and `disabled` classNames keys
 (e.g. `"text-white/25 opacity-50"` and
 `"text-white/20 opacity-40 cursor-not-allowed"`).
 Without `showOutsideDays`, the cells render empty (blank grid slots).
 Without the `disabled` prop, outside days are visually dimmed but still
 selectable — the `classNames` key alone has no behavioral effect.

### react-day-picker — calendar navigation arrows
NEVER fight react-day-picker's internal nav classNames for arrow placement.
The library's nav/caption layout is inconsistent across versions and produces
different failure modes with every CSS attempt.

The correct pattern (confirmed working):
1. Add `hideNavigation` prop to <DayPicker> to suppress the built-in nav
2. Add `month={calendarMonth}` and `onMonthChange={setCalendarMonth}` for controlled month
3. Build a fully custom header above the <DayPicker> with our own prev/next buttons:
   - ChevronLeft / ChevronRight from lucide-react
   - month label via `calendarMonth.toLocaleDateString(locale, { month: "long", year: "numeric" })`
   - `capitalize` class on the label (French months are lowercase by default)
4. Hide the caption row: `month_caption: "hidden"` in classNames

This took 1 round. CSS-only approaches took 8+ rounds and never fully worked.
Already implemented in: components/coach-profile/booking-panel.tsx

### Onboarding carousel pattern (coach + client onboarding steps)

All onboarding steps use the shared `OnboardingShell` component from
`components/onboarding/onboarding-shell.tsx`. Never recreate inline shell
wrappers — always import the shared one.

**Slide carousel rules:**
- Use `AnimatePresence mode="wait" custom={direction}` from Framer Motion
- Direction: `1` = forward (slide from right), `-1` = backward (slide from left)
- Variants: enter x: "100%"/-"100%", center x: 0, exit x: "-100%"/"100%"
- Touch swipe: useRef for touchStartX, 50px threshold, wired to handleNext/handleBack
- CRITICAL: wrap AnimatePresence in `<div className="overflow-hidden">` — prevents slide content escaping the card during x-axis transitions
- Never add an inner glass card inside OnboardingShell — it already provides the card

**Step indicator rules:**
- Translation key: `onboarding.step.of` = "Étape {current} sur {total}" / "Step {current} of {total}"
- Use `useTranslations("onboarding.step")` as a separate hook (tStep)
- Dots: current = `w-6 bg-teal-accent`, completed = `w-2 bg-teal-accent/40`, future = `w-2 bg-white/20`
- Always h-2 rounded-full on all dots

**Button variants in onboarding:**
- Next / Continue CTA → `Button variant="entry"` (blue) — NOT teal/primary
- Back chevron → native `<button>` with ChevronLeft icon, no border/bg, text-white/60
- Skip optional step → plain text button, text-white/30 hover:text-white/50
- All buttons: cursor-pointer, rounded-xl, focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-teal-accent/60

**Validation rules:**
- Validate per slide — disable Next when current slide invalid
- Required fields: show red asterisk (*), disable Next
- Optional fields: show "Optionnel" label, always valid (returns true)
- Min length fields: show character counter below input

**Reuse existing namespaces for labels:**
- Country labels: `search.countries.*`
- Language labels: `languages.*`
- Never hardcode country/language names in component arrays

**Client onboarding — 6-step pattern:**
- 6 standalone pages under `app/[locale]/onboarding/client/`
- Route order: /onboarding/client (name) → avatar → location → level → languages → goal → done
- Each page: OnboardingShell + back chevron + 6 dots + title + content + 3-element nav row
- 3-element nav row: [ChevronLeft button] [Button variant="entry" flex-1] [ChevronRight button]
- ChevronLeft/Right: size-11, rounded-xl, border-white/10, bg-white/5, disabled=opacity-25
- ChevronRight duplicates the Continue action — same onClick, same disabled state
- Store: `lib/stores/onboarding-client-store.ts` — Zustand persist, key `swimai-client-onboarding`
- Actions: setStep1(displayName) → setStep2(avatarType, avatarUrl) → setStep3(location, country) → setStep4(level) → setStep5(languages) → setStep6(goal) → reset()
- Types: `types/client.ts` — ClientGoal, ClientLevel, ClientOnboardingData

**Avatar step — two options only:**
- Default: show user initials (first 2 chars of displayName) on teal/20 background
- Upload: file input → FileReader → base64 → store as avatarUrl
- NO external avatar APIs (DiceBear v10 HTTP API silently ignores component params)

**Language badges — text only, no flags:**
- LANGUAGES array of 10 codes: fr, en, es, de, it, pt, ar, zh, ru, ja
- Use `useTranslations("languages")` for labels
- Max 4 languages enforced with counter X/4
- Text-only badges: rounded-xl border px-4 py-2
- Selected: border-teal-accent/40 bg-teal-accent/10 text-teal-accent
- Unselected: border-white/10 bg-white/5 text-white/60

**Coach listing card pattern:**
- Avatar: size-28 sm:size-32, rounded-md, ring-2 ring-white/10
- Badges: text-only pills, no dot separator between them
- Bottom row: location (teal MapPin) + price on same line
- CTA: full-width Button variant="primary" (teal) below location/price row

**DiceBear — DO NOT USE via HTTP API for specific options:**
The DiceBear v10 HTTP API silently ignores component-specific params
(topType, facialHairType, hair, etc.) — only seed and backgroundColor work
reliably. If avatar customization is needed, use the npm package
@dicebear/core + @dicebear/collection with correct v10 API, or use
initials-based default avatars.

### Scrollbar — canonical utility
- Use `scrollbar-thin` (defined in app/globals.css) for ALL scrollable
 containers that should show a visible scrollbar:
 - Dropdown panels (Popover.Popup content)
 - Any overflow-y-auto container that needs a visible bar
 - Pattern: `className="max-h-56 overflow-y-auto scrollbar-thin"`
- Use `scrollbar-none [&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden [-ms-overflow-style:none]
 [scrollbar-width:none]` ONLY when:
 - A custom JS <Scrollbar /> overlay is present
 (coaches-listing-client.tsx sidebar)
 - Mobile drawer scroll areas
- NEVER use inline arbitrary scrollbar classes — always use scrollbar-thin
 or scrollbar-none.

## General
- Before adding a new dependency, check if shadcn/ui, lucide-react, or an
 existing util already covers the need.
- Run `pnpm build` locally before considering a feature "done" — it must build
 with zero errors.

## Data / API
- NEVER import `coaches` array directly from `lib/coaches` or `lib/coaches-data`
  in any new component or page. Always use the API functions:
  - `getAllCoaches()` — get all coaches
  - `getCoachById(id)` — get one coach by id
  - `getCoachesByFilters(filters)` — get filtered coaches
  - `getCoachReviews(coachId)` — get reviews for a coach
- These functions currently read from static data but will be replaced
  with real database calls during the database sprint.
- Direct data imports bypass the API layer and break database migration.
- Exception: `lib/coaches-data.ts` itself imports the raw array — that is
 the ONLY file allowed to do so.

**Client types location:**
- Client types live in `types/client.ts` — ClientGoal, ClientLevel, ClientOnboardingData
- Client onboarding store: `lib/stores/onboarding-client-store.ts`
- Coach onboarding store: `lib/stores/onboarding-coach-store.ts`
- Never inline onboarding data shapes in component files

## Spacing/layout disputes — measure, don't calculate
- When a visual spacing/gap issue persists across 2+ rounds of `className`
 value changes that don't match the rendered result, STOP recalculating from
 source code. Source-level math (padding + margin + line-height + parent
 overflow, etc.) frequently fails to predict real rendered spacing, especially
 with sticky/floating elements, custom line-heights, or multi-layer wrapper
 components.
- Instead: take a screenshot of the actual rendered page, measure the gap
directly from the image (even approximately), and apply ONE direct value
change based on that real measurement. This converges in 1-2 rounds;
continued on-paper recalculation can drag on indefinitely without ever
matching what's actually on screen.

### Sticky positioning — verify with live pixel measurement, not Computed tab alone
- `position: sticky` can show as `sticky` in DevTools' Computed tab
while NOT actually behaving as sticky (moving 1:1 with scroll,
identical to `static`). The Computed tab confirms the CSS VALUE was
applied; it does NOT confirm the element is actually pinning itself
during scroll. Do not trust "Computed says sticky" as proof the
bug is fixed.
- The decisive test: log `document.querySelector(selector).getBoundingClientRect().top`
at `scrollY: 0` and again after scrolling ~100px. If `top` changed
by roughly the same amount as `scrollY` did, the element is NOT
sticking — something is blocking it even though the CSS class is
present.
- Common real cause: the sticky element's PARENT container is sized
to exactly the sticky element's own height (e.g. a `<>` fragment or
wrapper that doesn't extend down with the rest of the page content).
`position: sticky` only has "room" to stick within its nearest
scrolling ancestor — if that ancestor is no taller than the sticky
element itself, there's no scroll distance for it to stick across,
and it behaves like a normal in-flow element for the entire page
height.
- The fix is structural, not a CSS value change: ensure the sticky
element and the actual long-scrolling page content are true
siblings within a container that extends the full page height — not
one nested inside a wrapper sized only to the sticky element's own
content.
- This is a DIFFERENT failure mode from the earlier-documented
"ancestor `overflow: hidden` breaks sticky" issue — both produce
the same visible symptom (element doesn't stick) but have different
causes and different fixes. Check BOTH: (1) any ancestor `overflow-*`
declaration, AND (2) whether the immediate parent's height actually
extends with the scrollable content.
- The sticky element must be a DIRECT CHILD of a container that extends with
  the scrollable page content. A common mistake: putting `sticky` on an inner
  card div whose parent `<aside>` is only as tall as the card itself — the
  aside has no scroll distance for the card to pin across, so sticky behaves
  like static. The correct fix is structural: move `sticky top-X` to the
  `<aside>` itself, which IS a direct child of the long-scrolling flex row.

## Pre-flight checklist — required before every Cursor prompt

Before sending any prompt to Cursor that introduces new JSX, Claude must
verify each of the following. Any item that fails must be corrected in
the prompt before it is sent:

### Interactive elements
- Every new `<button>`, `<a>`, or clickable element has `cursor-pointer`
- Every new `<button>` has `rounded-xl` — never square, never `rounded-full`
- Every new `<button>` has `focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-teal-accent/60`
- Disabled buttons keep their focus ring — do not add `disabled:focus-visible:ring-0`

### Strings
- Every user-visible string goes through `useTranslations()` — no hardcoded
  French or English text in JSX, including `aria-label`, `placeholder`,
  `title`, and fallback expressions like `|| "default text"`
- Every new key added to `fr.json` has a matching key added to `en.json`
  in the same change
- No `as any` casts on translation key paths — if the path feels wrong,
  use a separate `useTranslations()` hook for the correct namespace

### Colors and tokens
- No raw hex or oklch values in `.tsx` files — use design tokens from
  `app/globals.css` only (e.g. `bg-teal-accent`, not `bg-[#00B5A4]`)

### Suspense
- Any new page component that calls (or renders a child that calls)
  `useSearchParams()` must wrap that component in `<Suspense fallback={null}>`

### Verification
- Every prompt ends with explicit browser verification instructions —
  not just `pnpm build`, but specific things to click, interact with,
  and visually confirm before closing the checkpoint
- Claude never asks to commit until browser verification is confirmed
